{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cght5aJX3jiFpParkLkW1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fitted the three bands with the full d³ octahedral ligand-field matrices and checked the result numerically with the term-energy tool.\n\n**1. Dq from the ⁴A₂g → ⁴T₂g band.** For d³, the energy of ⁴T₂g above the ground state is exactly 10Dq, so 10Dq = 26 600 cm⁻¹ and **Dq = 2660 cm⁻¹**.\n\n**2. B from the ⁴A₂g → ⁴T₁g(F) band.**\nE(⁴T₁g) = 15Dq + 7.5B − ½·√(225B² − 180B·Dq + 100Dq²) = 32 500 cm⁻¹\n\n- Rearranging gives √(225B² − 478 800B + 26 600²) = 14 800 + 15B.\n- Squaring both sides cancels the B² terms: 707 560 000 − 478 800B = 219 040 000 + 444 000B.\n- So **B ≈ 529 cm⁻¹**.\n- Check: with Dq = 2660 and B = 529.4, the full calculation gives ⁴T₁g at 32 500 cm⁻¹.\n\n**3. C from the spin-forbidden ⁴A₂g → ²Eg band.**\n- The ²Eg energy depends mainly on C. A rough strong-field estimate is E(²E) ≈ 9B + 3C − 90B²/10Dq, which gives C ≈ 3000 cm⁻¹.\n- I then refined C with the full d³ matrices:\n  - C = 3000 gives ²E at 13 195 cm⁻¹.\n  - C = 2865 gives ²E at 12 787 cm⁻¹.\n  - The ²E energy rises by about 3.0 cm⁻¹ for each 1 cm⁻¹ increase in C. Interpolating to the observed 12 820 cm⁻¹ gives **C ≈ 2876 cm⁻¹**.\n\nChanging C does not move the quartet bands. With these parameters the calculation also predicts ²T₁g at about 13 150 cm⁻¹ and ²T₂g at about 20 200 cm⁻¹.\n\n**Result:** Dq = 2660 cm⁻¹, B ≈ 529 cm⁻¹, C ≈ 2876 cm⁻¹ (C/B ≈ 5.4). B is about 58% of the free-ion value for Cr³⁺ (about 918 cm⁻¹). This large drop in B is typical of the covalent Cr–CN bonding in cyanide complexes.\n\n{\"Dq\": 2660, \"B\": 529, \"C\": 2876, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Dq from 4T2, B from 4T1 via the d3 secular equation, C refined from 2E using full d3 matrices.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2200,"cache_read_input